    restMinutes: 15,
    donenessCue: 'Glaze is glossy and lightly caramelized in patches, the ham is heated through to 140°F, and the cut faces of the spiral slices are barely beginning to weep juices — pull it before they dry out.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'A spiral ham dries out by the minute once the slices open up. Keep it tented for the first hour so it heats gently and stays moist, then take the foil off and brush on glaze in three coats over the last 20–30 minutes. One thick coat at the start burns; three thin coats build a lacquered finish.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS guidance for reheating fully-cooked ham: 140°F internal at 10–15 minutes per pound at 325°F for a bone-in whole or half ham. This is a reheat, not a cook — the ham was already brought to a safe temperature at the packing plant, so the target is heat-through, not the 145°F fresh-pork minimum.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Because this ham was already fully cooked at the packing plant, 325°F here is only raising temperature, not driving further protein change — which is why the target is a modest 140°F rather than a texture-based doneness point. Tenting with foil for the first hour traps the steam the ham naturally sheds as it warms; since turning liquid water into vapor consumes a large amount of energy, that trapped humidity slows further evaporative loss from the exposed spiral faces far more than an open pan would. The bone at the center is denser and slower to heat than the surrounding meat, which is the real reason the per-pound timing runs longer than a boneless roast of the same weight.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'sheet-pan-glazed-ham',
    keywords: ['how long to bake spiral ham', 'how long to heat spiral ham per pound', 'spiral ham temperature usda', 'sheet pan ham time', 'glazed ham oven temperature'],
