    proTip: 'Line the pan with foil and start with a cold oven. Cold-start bacon renders more evenly because the fat starts liquefying before the meat begins to seize and curl, and the foil turns cleanup from a 20-minute soak into a crumpled ball in the trash.',
    verificationBasis: 'Cured supermarket bacon carries no USDA FSIS minimum internal temperature — it is judged by rendered-fat and crisp-texture cues rather than a probe reading, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 400°F/18–22 minute window reflects standard oven-bacon technique for regular-cut strips on a rimmed half-sheet; thick-cut adds 4–6 minutes.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Bacon\'s fat cap starts liquefying well before the meat surface reaches Maillard temperatures, so at 400°F the fat renders out early and pools beneath each strip, effectively shallow-frying the underside while radiant and convective heat brown the top independently. Curing salts have already altered the muscle proteins before this bacon ever reaches the oven, which changes how quickly the exposed lean streaks color relative to an uncured cut of the same thickness. Laid flat and unstacked rather than overlapped, every strip keeps full contact with either hot metal or open air, so the collagen bands holding each strip together soften evenly along its whole length instead of only where it happens to touch the pan.',
    keywords: ['how long to bake bacon', 'sheet pan bacon time', 'bacon oven temperature', 'oven bacon 400', 'baked bacon time'],
