    restMinutes: 0,
    donenessCue: 'Spears are bright green with lightly browned tips and the thickest part of the stalk gives just slightly when picked up — a spear that hangs limp from the middle is overcooked, and one still rigid from tip to tip is under.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Snap, don\'t cut. Bend a spear near the bottom until it breaks on its own — that break point is exactly where the woody, stringy part ends and the tender part begins. Cutting every stalk at the same length either leaves fibrous inches behind or wastes an inch of good asparagus.',
    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — asparagus is judged by color, tip browning, and a slight bend in the stalk, so the field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 425°F/10–14 minute window reflects standard high-heat roasting for medium spears (roughly 3/8-inch thick); pencil-thin asparagus finishes in 6–8 minutes and jumbo spears take 15–18.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A medium spear is thin enough that its core and surface reach similar temperatures almost together, so cooking here is less about heat penetrating inward and more about how much surface moisture evaporates before the tips scorch. Bright green color depends on limiting that exposure, since chlorophyll degrades and dulls toward olive under prolonged heat, so the ten-to-fourteen-minute window at 425°F browns the tips through direct convective contact while most of the stalk stays vivid. No flip is needed because the spear\'s narrow diameter lets radiant heat from above and conducted heat from the pan below meet near the center well before either face overcooks.',
