    proTip: 'Undercook the pasta by a full minute before it goes into the béchamel — it will keep absorbing sauce and finish in the smoker, but pasta boiled to package-time turns to porridge by the time the crust sets. Smoked gouda is where the smoke flavour actually lives; if you cannot find it, add a teaspoon of smoked paprika to the béchamel rather than swapping in a different sharp cheese.',
    verificationBasis: 'Traeger Pro Series published mac-and-cheese cooking guide (275°F for 60–75 mins in cast iron); USDA FSIS 165°F casserole safe minimum.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'The roux beneath this béchamel does mechanical work plain melted cheese cannot: gelatinized starch swells into a network that physically traps water and fat, which keeps three cheeses\' worth of fat from breaking into a greasy pool during a 60 to 75 minute smoke, something a straight cheese sauce lacks the scaffolding to do. The panko topping browns faster than the custard-like center below because it sits directly exposed to 275°F convective heat with almost no moisture buffering it, while the dense starch-and-dairy matrix underneath conducts heat much more slowly inward. The 165°F target confirms heat has fully penetrated to the pan\'s center, not merely browned the top, since this low-conductivity mixture can look finished well before it actually is.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'one-pot-dutch-oven-mac-and-cheese',
    keywords: ['how long to smoke mac and cheese', 'smoked mac and cheese time', 'smoker mac and cheese temp', 'cast iron smoked mac', 'pellet grill mac and cheese'],
