    technicalDeepDive: 'The stall between the dry smoke and the foil wrap happens because evaporating surface moisture pulls latent heat away from the meat nearly as fast as the smoker adds it, holding the internal temperature near 165°F rather than letting it climb steadily. Wrapping in foil traps steam against the meat and removes that evaporative cooling, so the same 200°F ambient heat now transfers by steam condensation rather than slow convection through dry air, and the temperature resumes its climb toward 203°F. That last stretch matters because the collagen running through a flat cut like this only finishes converting to gelatin near the top of that range, well past where the beef itself is already safe.',
    keywords: ['how long to smoke pastrami', 'smoked pastrami time', 'corned beef pastrami smoker', 'homemade pastrami temp', 'smoked pastrami internal temp'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook pastrami in the smoker: 225°F (107°C) for 8–10 hours. Internal target 203°F–205°F (probe tender). Verified times, no guesswork.',
