    restMinutes: 1,
    donenessCue: 'Blistered browned skin, crisp-tender snap.',
    oilSprayRequired: true,
    proTip: 'Mist with olive oil and dust with garlic powder and sea salt before air frying.',
    verificationBasis: 'Instant Vortex Plus & Manufacturer Technical Guide; USDA FSIS Safe Food Handling Standard.',
    uniqueEquipmentNotes: [
      'Beans are short and round, so they tumble and tangle into loose clumps rather than lying flat the way a longer vegetable would — a real toss that flips the whole pile over is needed partway through, since a gentle shake mostly just resettles the same tangled clumps in place.',
      'Apply the oil mist first and the garlic powder and salt after, not the reverse — dry powder dusted onto bare, dry beans mostly sifts through to the basket floor, while it clings properly once the surface is already tacky with oil.',
      'Twelve ounces of trimmed beans fills roughly one uncrowded layer in a standard family-size basket; in a smaller personal-size unit, split it into two shorter batches instead of mounding it all in, since a compact basket can\'t spread that volume into a single layer.',
      'Coarse sea salt lands as fewer, larger crystals with more bare space between them than fine table salt, so expect a spottier seasoning pattern that evens out somewhat as the crystals partially dissolve into surface oil and released moisture during cooking.',
      'Cook beans on a perforated basket or mesh tray rather than a solid crisper plate — beans resting directly on solid metal lose bottom-side airflow entirely and steam against the plate instead of blistering on that side.',
    ],
    uniqueSensoryCues: [
      'Listen near the end of the cook for a scattered, popcorn-like series of small pops across the basket, which is the cuticle rupturing at multiple points on different pods rather than one uniform sound.',
      'Look for irregular tan-brown freckling scattered across the pods rather than a smooth, even tan — since ruptures happen wherever the waxy coating happens to give first, the browning pattern is naturally blotchy, not uniform.',
      'Bend a bean between two fingers — it should give slightly and fracture with a light snap, distinct from a raw bean\'s sharp, resistant snap with almost no bend, and from an overcooked bean that folds over with no crack at all.',
      'Check the overall sheen — properly cooked beans look slightly matte and shriveled along their length as surface moisture vents off, in contrast to the glossy, taut skin of the raw, unseasoned pod.',
    ],
    uniqueFailureModes: [
      {
        mistake: 'Tossing beans with oil while they\'re still damp from rinsing.',
        consequence: 'The residual water dilutes the oil coating and creates steam right at the surface, so instead of blistering the pods stay a dull, limp, army-green.',
        prevention: 'Pat beans fully dry with a towel before tossing with oil.',
      },
      {
        mistake: 'Dusting on a heavy, uneven layer of garlic powder, especially where beans overlap.',
        consequence: 'Ground garlic powder scorches and turns bitter well before the vegetable itself finishes at 380°F, since dry powder heats and burns faster than the moist bean surface underneath it.',
        prevention: 'Use a light, even dusting and toss the beans afterward to distribute it rather than concentrating it in one spot.',
      },
      {
        mistake: 'Leaving the tough stem nub attached instead of trimming it off.',
        consequence: 'That woody little end doesn\'t blister or soften the way the rest of the pod does, so it stays stringy and fibrous no matter how long the batch cooks.',
        prevention: 'Trim the stem end off every pod, not just the ones where it looks obviously tough.',
      },
      {
        mistake: 'Loading beans straight from the refrigerator into an already-preheated basket.',
        consequence: 'The temperature difference can cause a thin layer of condensation to form on the cold surface the instant it meets the warm, humid basket air, undermining the oil coating right as cooking starts and delaying blistering.',
        prevention: 'Let beans sit out briefly to lose the chill before tossing and loading, or load them while the basket is still coming up to temperature.',
      },
      {
        mistake: 'Using a flavored or additive-based nonstick spray instead of plain oil.',
        consequence: 'Beans have a lot of surface area relative to their mass, so any gummy or off-flavor residue in the spray concentrates noticeably on them compared to how it would read on a denser food.',
        prevention: 'Use a plain oil mist rather than a flavored or heavily additive-laden spray.',
      },
      {
        mistake: 'Adding the garlic powder and salt partway through the cook, after a mid-cook shake, rather than at the start.',
        consequence: 'By that point the surface oil has mostly cooked into the skin rather than sitting wet on top, so the dry seasoning has little to cling to and much of it ends up loose on the basket floor.',
        prevention: 'Season before the beans go in, while the oil coating is still fully wet.',
      },
    ],
    bonusFaqs: [
      {
        q: 'Can I use canned green beans instead of fresh?',
        a: 'Not really with this method — canned beans are already fully cooked and soft from the canning liquid, so air frying them just dries and toughens the exterior unevenly rather than producing the blistered skin you get from fresh pods.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Should I blanch the beans in boiling water first like a lot of stovetop recipes suggest?',
        a: 'No — blanching adds moisture back into the pod right before you\'re trying to drive moisture off and blister the skin, so it works against the air fryer method rather than helping it.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can I use fresh minced garlic instead of garlic powder?',
        a: 'You can, but fresh garlic has more moisture and sugar than the dried powder and burns noticeably faster at 380°F, so if you use it, add it partway through the cook rather than at the start to avoid a bitter char.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Some of my beans came out perfectly blistered and others still bright green and crunchy-raw in the same batch — why?',
        a: 'That\'s usually a sign of tangled clumps shielding some pods from direct airflow, or a bag with a wider size range than usual, with noticeably thinner haricot-vert-style pods mixed in with standard ones cooking at a different rate.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Do I need to trim both ends of the bean, or just one?',
        a: 'Just the stem end typically needs trimming, since that\'s the tough, fibrous attachment point; the tapered tip end is thin enough to cook through fine and doesn\'t need to be cut off.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Is the browned, blistered skin safe to eat, or should it be avoided?',
        a: 'It\'s fine to eat — that browning is just the same Maillard reaction that colors toast or a seared steak, not the acrid, blackened char you\'d get from actually burning the pod.',
      },
    ],
    technicalDeepDive: 'Green beans carry a waxy cuticle over a thin, fibrous pod, and that cuticle briefly resists moisture loss even as the surrounding air holds steady at 380°F. Once it breaches, trapped moisture flashes to vapor right at the surface, and the resulting small ruptures are exactly where blistering and browning concentrate, since a broken cuticle exposes sugars and amino acids directly to the hot moving air. The slightly lower set point than a starchier vegetable keeps that transition gradual enough for the interior fibers to soften through pectin breakdown before the skin scorches. Because the pods are so thin, conduction from skin to core is nearly instant, so the bean finishes almost the moment the surface browns.',
