    technicalDeepDive: 'A whole packer brisket fuses two muscles, the flat and the point, along a thick seam of collagen and fat that only breaks down given hours at low, steady heat, which is why even the upper end of this range still runs half a day. Because raw meat conducts heat poorly, the exterior of a roast this large can sit well above target for hours while the center lags behind, so the low smoker temperature also protects the bark from burning while conduction slowly carries heat inward. Wrapping in porous butcher paper at the stall vents some steam while still blocking enough evaporative cooling to push the brisket on toward 203°F, unlike a fully sealed wrap, which would leave the bark soft rather than set.',

