    technicalDeepDive: 'A hot dog is already a fully cooked, pasteurized emulsion of finely ground meat, fat, and water, so the 400°F oven isn\'t cooking it in any structural sense, it\'s carrying every point of that cylindrical mass above 165°F for a safe reheat. That same emulsified structure traps water and fat under the casing, and as it warms, steam builds pressure with nowhere planned to go, which is exactly what the diagonal scores are there to vent. Because the mass is small and uniform, convective heat penetrates it quickly, and flipping at the 7-minute mark exposes the underside to the same airflow so both faces blister evenly rather than one side staying pale.',
