    technicalDeepDive: 'Milling strips the bran and germ from white rice, exposing starchy endosperm directly to water, but brown rice keeps that outer pericarp and aleurone layer intact — mostly lipid and fiber rather than starch, material water has to diffuse through before ever reaching a granule capable of gelatinizing. That diffusion proceeds at its own pace no matter how hard the pot boils, since water already sits at its 212°F ceiling the moment it starts bubbling, leaving no extra thermal energy to push into a process governed by molecular migration through a fibrous coating rather than available heat. That intact bran layer, not the starch underneath it, is what sets the pace for the entire simmer.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook brown rice', 'brown rice stovetop time', 'boiling brown rice', 'brown rice water ratio', 'long grain brown rice cooking time'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook long-grain brown rice: 45–50 min simmer + 10 min rest, 2 1/4:1 water ratio. Verified against Lundberg Family Farms instructions.',
