    technicalDeepDive: 'An eight-egg custard this thick needs heat to travel much farther than a thin one does, and conduction from the cast iron alone would fully set and toughen the eggs touching the metal long before that heat finishes migrating up to the center\'s 160°F egg-dish threshold. Moving the pan under the broiler adds a second heat source working from the opposite direction: radiant heat cooks the top down while stored heat in the iron keeps cooking the bottom up, so the two fronts meet somewhere in the middle instead of one side doing all the work alone. Dairy folded into the eggs also raises the temperature at which the proteins fully coagulate, which buys a little extra margin against a rubbery center.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'cast-iron-loaded-frittata',
    keywords: ['how long to cook frittata cast iron', 'cast iron frittata temperature', 'frittata internal temp', 'why is my frittata rubbery', 'cast iron frittata broiler'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook a frittata in cast iron: 10–14 minutes, low stovetop then broiler. Why cast-iron carryover means pulling it barely under-set.',
