    technicalDeepDive: 'Wings pack a high ratio of skin to lean mass, and that skin holds a fat layer that needs sustained time at moderate heat to render out fully rather than just seizing rubbery. The 375°F indirect phase supplies steady convective heat that melts this fat slowly while the dry-brined surface loses moisture, setting up the final direct blast to actually crisp the skin through Maillard browning instead of just drying it. Pulling well past the 165°F safety floor into the 175–195°F range matters because the thin collagen sheath around wing joints and small bones needs that extra exposure to convert to gelatin; stopped at 165°F, that collagen stays tight and the bite resists rather than pulling clean.',
