    technicalDeepDive: 'Blotting the skin dry matters because residual surface water has to evaporate before the skin can reach browning temperatures, and its collagen needs sustained direct heat to dehydrate and crisp rather than steam and turn rubbery. Fish muscle is built from short myotomes separated by thin sheets of connective tissue that convert to gelatin at far lower temperatures than beef or pork collagen does, which is why salmon flakes and turns opaque well below the internal temperatures familiar from red meat. Keeping the fillet skin-down for most of the cook lets direct pan conduction do the bulk of the work, while the flesh above finishes gently off residual heat rather than direct contact.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'oven-baked-salmon-fillet',
    keywords: ['how long to cook salmon in skillet', 'pan seared salmon time', 'crispy skin salmon on stove', 'skillet salmon internal temp', 'how to cook skin on salmon fillet'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook salmon in a skillet: 7–9 min over medium heat. The 90/10 skin-down rule for glass-crispy skin.',
