    technicalDeepDive: 'Fish collagen is chemically different from the collagen in beef or chicken and breaks down into gelatin at a much lower temperature, which is why salmon can go from raw to fully tender within just a few degrees instead of needing a long braise — pulling at 125–130°F denatures the muscle proteins and softens that connective tissue in the same narrow window. The skin sits over a distinct fat layer that buffers the 450°F grate from the delicate flesh above it, conducting heat more gently than bare flesh would and buying the six-to-eight-minute skin-down time without scorching the fish through. Cooking past roughly 140°F pushes albumin, the protein responsible for the white substance that seeps from overcooked salmon, to coagulate and weep out of the flesh, well past the medium pull this cook targets.',
