    verificationBasis: 'No internal temperature target — portobellos are a texture-and-colour finish, so internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2, consistent with other vegetable entries in this dataset. Direct-heat time range and gill-removal guidance from the Weber Grilling Guide grilled-vegetables chart and America\'s Test Kitchen grilled-portobello technique.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Portobello flesh is fungal tissue held together by chitin-based cell walls rather than the collagen and fiber that govern meat cookery, so there is no denaturation threshold here; doneness is turgor loss, as radiant heat ruptures water-filled cells and the cap collapses from a firm dome into something floppy. Because the cap is mostly water by volume, most of the 425°F energy hitting it goes into driving off that moisture rather than raising any internal temperature far, which is also why there is no useful internal number to chase. The char that forms toward the end comes from fungal sugars concentrating at the surface as water leaves, caramelizing once the exterior finally dries enough to brown.',
