    technicalDeepDive: 'A T-bone carries two separate muscles, a strip loin and a smaller tenderloin, sharing one bone, and because they differ in marbling and fiber density they don\'t approach the same internal temperature at the same rate under identical fire. The 550°F-plus direct blast pushes both cut faces through the Maillard range quickly, since browning depends on surface temperature rather than the internal doneness target. Sliding to the cooler 350°F zone afterward lets conduction carry heat inward gradually, and because muscle proteins tighten and squeeze out moisture progressively as they climb toward the pull point rather than all at once, a slower finish holds more juice in the strip lobe than finishing entirely over direct flame would.',
    keywords: ['how long to grill t-bone steak', 't-bone grill time', 't-bone steak internal temperature', 'grilled t-bone medium rare', 't-bone two-zone grill'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to grill a T-bone: two-zone 550°F direct + 350°F indirect, 10–14 min to a 125°F pull. Why the tenderloin lobe faces the cool side.',
