    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 165°F safe minimum for whole poultry; 175°F preferred for dark-meat texture per Meathead Goldwyn\'s poultry temperature guide on AmazingRibs.com. Indirect method, temperature, and time range from the Weber Grilling Guide beer-can-chicken chart and Steven Raichlen\'s tested beer-can-chicken technique (BBQ USA).',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Breast and thigh are different muscles with very different collagen content, which is why they are judged to different points: 165°F fully sets the lean breast without drying it further, while the thigh\'s greater connective tissue needs to reach 175°F before the joint stops feeling tough. Cooking over an indirect zone with a drip pan also changes the fire\'s chemistry: keeping rendered skin fat away from direct flame means it vaporizes and gets swept off by convection instead of hitting coals and combusting into acrid smoke. The long uncovered dry brine beforehand draws moisture out through osmosis and lets it evaporate in the fridge, so the skin the heat finally meets is already dry enough to crisp quickly instead of losing surface water first.',
