    technicalDeepDive: 'Softening here is a pectin story rather than a protein one: the middle lamella cementing apple cells together is built from pectin that gradually hydrolyzes under sustained heat and the mild acidity of the lemon juice, and once enough of those bonds break, the cells separate from each other and the fruit collapses rather than merely going soft at the surface. Because a slow cooker\'s liquid environment never approaches the dry, radiant heat that drives browning, none of these sugars ever caramelize the way they would in a hot pan; the rosy tint instead comes from pigment compounds in the peel slowly leaching into the liquid.',
