    restMinutes: 15,
    donenessCue: 'A chuck cube pressed with the back of a spoon splits along the grain with almost no push; carrots are fully soft but hold their shape at the wall of a bowl; potatoes have taken on a beige-brown tint from the braising liquid and are fork-tender through the centre.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Sear the chuck in three uncrowded batches before it hits the crock — one big pile of raw cubes dumped straight in gives you gray stew meat and a watery broth, because a slow cooker never gets hot enough to develop the Maillard crust you need for the dark, deep colour that reads as "stew" instead of "boiled beef".',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 145°F + 3-min rest whole-cut beef safe minimum; Certified Angus Beef "Braising Guide" (chuck reaches probe-tender collagen breakdown at 200–205°F internal); Crock-Pot 6-Qt Set & Forget cooking chart (beef stew LOW 7–8 hrs); ATK "Slow Cooker Revolution" beef-stew method (sear first, then LOW 7–9 hrs). Root-vegetable timing verified against Cook\'s Illustrated slow-cooker root-vegetable notes.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Chuck, potato, and carrot run on different internal clocks in the same pot: potato cells hold their gelatinized starch inside intact walls, so a Yukon Gold turns creamy within while still holding its shape, carrot\'s denser, more fibrous walls need sustained exposure before their pectin loosens enough to yield, and chuck\'s collagen needs longest of all, since gelatin conversion depends on hours at a steady simmer rather than on how hot that simmer runs. Once the muscle fibers in the chuck have tightened and released their own moisture, it\'s that slowly forming gelatin, along with rendered fat, that keeps the meat from tasting dry despite the long cook.',
    keywords: ['how long to slow cook beef stew', 'beef stew slow cooker time', 'crockpot beef stew', 'slow cooker beef stew internal temp', 'chuck beef stew low all day'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook classic beef stew in the slow cooker: 7–8 hours on LOW to a 200°F chuck. Why searing the beef in three batches actually matters.',
