    technicalDeepDive: 'Pork shoulder is a heavily worked muscle laced with dense collagen, and the eight-to-ten-hour LOW cycle keeps it in the appliance\'s low, steady heat band long enough for that collagen to hydrolyze fully into gelatin rather than merely soften. Splitting the roast into three chunks shortens the distance heat travels inward from the crock wall, so the core catches up instead of lagging behind the bone\'s insulating mass. Citrus acid works on surface proteins throughout the long hold, loosening fiber structure as gelatin accumulates, and because the sealed lid keeps that released liquid circulating instead of evaporating off, the meat ends up self-basted rather than simply simmered.',
