    technicalDeepDive: 'Because the pan and bacon warm together from a cold start, fat that renders out early pools beneath each strip into a shallow layer of hot liquid fat that conducts heat into the meat from below, rather than dry iron doing all the work. The lean muscle fibers end up cooking through a combination of convection through that liquid fat and direct conduction from the pan, a gentler path than dropping a room-temperature strip onto an already-screaming surface. The gradual ramp also means the surface only crosses into the Maillard range once most of the fat has already liquefied, so the browning happens after the connective tissue holding the fat has broken down rather than while it\'s still solid.',
