    technicalDeepDive: 'Patting the skin fully dry before salting matters because any surface water left behind has to absorb energy as latent heat of vaporization before the skin can get any hotter, which would stall it near the boiling point instead of letting it climb into the Maillard range during the sear. Once the breast moves to the oven, cooking shifts from the pan\'s direct conduction to slower convective and radiant heat that reaches the thick, lean meat more gently. That shift also gives the bone time to act as an internal heat sink: bone conducts heat more sluggishly than muscle, so the meat closest to it stays cooler longer, buffering that region against the moisture loss lean breast meat is otherwise prone to over an extended oven finish.',
