    donenessCue: 'The rendered fat runs clear amber (not milky pink); the pan bottom has a dark fond that has not burned; a probe pushed into a crumble anywhere in the pan reads 160°F, and there is no visible pink meat when you spread the crumble flat.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Most people wreck browned beef by stirring immediately — you dump the meat in, panic-stir, and the pan drops below the searing point so you end up steaming grey pebbles in their own liquid. Leave it alone for the first 3 minutes and press it flat so the bottom face develops a crust, then break the crust up with a stiff spatula and let the exposed faces sear before you stir again.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS 160°F ground beef minimum internal temperature (no rest allowance — ground meat must reach the temperature during the cook, not after). Undisturbed-then-break browning method per Serious Eats "The Food Lab" ground-beef technique (J. Kenji López-Alt) and America\'s Test Kitchen taco meat reference — both attribute the deeper flavor of properly browned ground beef to Maillard fond that only forms above 300°F pan-surface contact.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Grinding meat multiplies its surface area and mixes any surface bacteria into what becomes the interior of every chunk, which is why USDA requires ground beef to hit its target temperature throughout with no rest allowance, unlike a whole muscle cut where contamination stays on the surface. That same increase in surface area means ground beef releases moisture readily, and if it pools under a crowded, frequently stirred pile, it sits near the boiling point and absorbs energy as latent heat of vaporization instead of letting the pan climb into the Maillard range. Keeping the meat pressed flat and undisturbed lets that moisture evaporate off as steam rather than pool, so the exposed face can actually reach browning temperature instead of stalling near 212°F.',
