    donenessCue: 'The pancake has climbed 3–4 inches up the sides of the skillet with a canyon of steam channels through its cratered top; the edges are a deep golden brown and start to collapse within 60 seconds of hitting room air.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'A Dutch baby rises from steam, not baking powder, and the steam only forms if the pan and butter are ripping hot when the cold batter hits — that thermal shock is the whole trick. Every time someone tells you their Dutch baby "didn\'t puff", the pan was not hot enough or they opened the oven door in the first 15 minutes and let the steam escape.',
    verificationBasis: 'Quickbread internal-doneness window of 200–205°F per America\'s Test Kitchen baking reference (starch gelatinisation for an egg-and-flour batter). 425°F oven temperature and 3:3:3 egg-to-milk-to-flour ratio per J. Kenji López-Alt "The Food Lab" Dutch baby entry and Serious Eats Dutch baby recipe; both attribute the rise entirely to steam expansion from the cold batter in a 400°F+ skillet.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A Dutch baby\'s rise is a race between two things: how fast the batter\'s water turns to expanding vapor and how fast the egg and flour proteins firm up enough to hold that expansion in place. Too slow a set and the structure sags as steam escapes; too fast a set and there\'s no time to stretch before rigidity locks in, which is why the batter is thin and egg-heavy rather than thick like a muffin batter; less structural resistance early on gives the vapor room to inflate before coagulation catches up. The target core temperature marks the same protein-coagulation and starch-gelatinization point any egg-and-flour batter needs to cross to hold its shape, which is why the centre still needs to reach it even after the rise looks finished.',
    keywords: ['how long to bake dutch baby cast iron', 'dutch baby internal temp', 'cast iron dutch baby time', 'why is my dutch baby flat', 'german pancake cast iron'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to bake a Dutch baby pancake in cast iron: 18–22 minutes at 425°F in a preheated skillet. Why opening the oven door kills the rise.',
