    verificationBasis: 'Serious Eats "Rich and Silky Pressure Cooker Split Pea Soup with Ham" (J. Kenji López-Alt, 2015 — 15-minute HP + full NPR benchmark, no-salt-until-cooked rule from bean chemistry); USDA FSIS Ham and Food Safety fact sheet (smoked ham hocks are fully cooked/cured, no probe target needed); no internal-temp target — HR-2 permits omitting internalTempTargetF for legume soups.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Split peas are dehulled and split before packaging, which strips away the tough seed coat that slows water uptake in a whole bean and exposes more surface area directly to water. That structural head start is why this pot needs only a fraction of the time a whole dried pinto needs to fully disintegrate. The ham hock supplies separate chemistry: its skin and tendon are dense with collagen, and the same elevated chamber temperature that gelatinizes the pea starch also hydrolyzes that collagen into gelatin faster than an open pot could manage, which is what thickens the broth into something that coats a spoon rather than staying watery.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook split pea soup in instant pot', 'instant pot split pea soup time', 'pressure cooker split pea soup', 'instant pot ham hock', 'split pea soup ham hock instant pot'],
