    donenessCue: 'Individual thighs separate cleanly from the block when nudged with tongs; the flesh at the thickest point registers 165°F on a Thermapen and shreds under the fork with no pink strands near the seam.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'A frozen slab needs the extra 3–4 minutes over fresh thighs (Instant Pot Official chart calls 8–10 min for fresh boneless thigh, 12–14 for frozen) because half the cycle goes to melting the block apart before pressure can penetrate. Do NOT stack a fresh block on top of a frozen one to save a run — the fresh layer overcooks to sawdust while the frozen core is still catching up.',
    verificationBasis: 'Instant Brands Official Cooking Time Chart (Poultry → Chicken thighs boneless: 8–10 min HP fresh, +3–4 min for frozen slab, published in the Duo/Duo Plus/Pro user manuals and on instantpot.com); USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum internal temperature; probe verification with ThermoWorks Thermapen One.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A frozen slab of thigh meat first has to absorb a large amount of latent heat converting its ice back to liquid water, and that phase change happens before the temperature can climb toward denaturation at all. That is why the frozen run needs several extra minutes rather than scaling linearly with the fresh-thigh time. Thigh muscle also carries more connective tissue and intramuscular fat than breast, and with no bone acting as a thermal buffer in this boneless cut, pressurized steam near 239°F conducts evenly inward from every side once the ice has cleared, bringing the whole block across the USDA\'s 165°F poultry floor at roughly the same moment.',
    keywords: ['how long to cook frozen chicken thighs in instant pot', 'instant pot frozen boneless chicken thighs', 'pressure cooker frozen chicken thighs', 'instant pot frozen chicken time', 'frozen chicken thighs pressure cooker'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook frozen boneless chicken thighs in the Instant Pot: 12–14 mins High Pressure + 10 min NPR. Pull at 165°F internal. No thaw needed.',
