    cutOrPrep: 'Batter portioned in scant 1/4-cup scoops into a preheated nonstick or seasoned cast-iron 12-inch skillet lightly greased with 1/2 tsp neutral oil (wiped nearly dry with paper towel); cooked in batches of 3–4 pancakes to avoid crowding',
    state: 'fresh',
    tempF: 375,
    tempC: 191,
    tempFormatted: 'Medium heat (surface 350°F–375°F, drop of water dances 2–3 seconds before evaporating)',
    timeMinMinutes: 4,
    timeMaxMinutes: 5,
    timeFormatted: '4–5 mins total per pancake (2–3 min first side until bubbles pop and edges look dry, 1.5–2 min second side)',
    flipAtMinutes: 3,
    restMinutes: 0,
    donenessCue: 'Bubbles have formed across the entire surface and the ones in the center have popped and stayed open; edges look matte-dry rather than wet; underside is uniformly golden-brown with no pale spots; the pancake springs back when pressed lightly in the center.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Wait for the bubbles in the middle to pop and stay open before you flip — flipping on the first bubble ring gives you a pale, gummy interior. The most common failure is a skillet that is too hot: if the first pancake is dark-brown outside and raw inside, pull the skillet off the burner for 60 seconds to drop the surface temperature, then continue at medium-low. A drop of water should dance across the surface for 2–3 seconds; if it sizzles away instantly, the pan is too hot.',
    verificationBasis: 'Lodge Cast Iron 12-inch skillet stovetop griddle guide (medium 350°F–375°F range for pancake batter); King Arthur Baking "Perfect Buttermilk Pancakes" formulation and doneness cues (bubbles-pop-and-stay-open flip signal); Serious Eats "The Food Lab: The Best Buttermilk Pancakes" (Kenji López-Alt, medium-heat batch cooking); no meat probe used — internalTempTargetF omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Buttermilk\'s acid begins reacting with the baking soda as soon as the batter is mixed, and the 350 to 375°F pan surface then supplies enough conductive heat to turn that reaction into bubbles rising visibly through the batter rather than a flat, dense round. Because the batter is mostly water, its interior can\'t climb much past 212°F until moisture drives off as steam, giving the flour\'s starches time to gelatinize and set the crumb before the center scorches. The pan face itself runs well above that ceiling, so it browns only the thin layer of batter touching it directly, which is why the crumb stays pale while the crust turns deep gold. Medium heat rather than high heat matters because it lets gelatinization finish before Maillard browning runs away on the exterior.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'skillet-fluffy-buttermilk-pancakes',
    keywords: ['how long to cook pancakes in a skillet', 'skillet buttermilk pancakes time', 'pancake stove temperature', 'when to flip pancakes', 'fluffy pancakes on stovetop'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook buttermilk pancakes in a skillet: 4–5 min per pancake at medium heat. Flip when the center bubbles pop and stay open.',
