    flipAtMinutes: 5,
    internalTempTargetF: 165,
    internalTempTargetFormatted: '165°F (74°C) — USDA FSIS chicken minimum, checked at the thickest part of the breast before topping with cheese',
    restMinutes: 5,
    donenessCue: 'Mozzarella has fully melted into glossy pools with the first hint of browning at the edges; marinara is bubbling actively around the perimeter of the pan; chicken reads 165°F on a Thermapen; no pink or opaque-uncooked spots visible where the cheese has parted.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Because there is no breading to protect the meat, sear the chicken to a hard golden crust in a screaming-hot skillet before the sauce goes in — that crust is where all the flavor lives. Confirm 165°F BEFORE piling on the cheese; a probe reading through molten mozzarella is nearly impossible, and covered-lid melting only adds 5–10°F to the meat. If the mozzarella needs help browning at the end, run the covered skillet under the broiler for 90 seconds with the lid off.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA FSIS "Chicken from Farm to Table" 165°F safe minimum; Lodge Cast Iron 12-inch skillet stovetop guide (medium-high 400°F sear range); Serious Eats "Easy Skillet Chicken Parmesan" (no-breading, covered-lid mozzarella melt method); ThermoWorks Thermapen ONE probe verification.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Butterflying the breast to a half inch shortens the path heat must conduct to the center, letting the 400°F sear push the surface through the Maillard range before the core has any chance to overshoot 165°F. Once the lid goes on, water vapor rising off the simmering marinara condenses against the cooler cheese and lid, releasing its stored latent heat directly onto the mozzarella, a far more efficient way to melt the top layer evenly than conduction from the sauce alone. Low-moisture mozzarella has had much of its whey pressed out during processing, so it liquefies into stretchy strands under that heat instead of weeping water into the marinara the way a wetter, fresh cheese would.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'one-pan-skillet-chicken-parm-no-breading',
    keywords: ['how long to cook chicken parm in a skillet', 'no breading chicken parmesan time', 'one pan chicken parm skillet', 'stovetop chicken parmesan internal temp', 'skillet chicken parm no oven'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to cook one-pan chicken parm (no breading) in a skillet: 20–25 min total. Sear at 400°F, hit 165°F, then covered melt of mozzarella.',
