    flipAtMinutes: 15,
    restMinutes: 0,
    donenessCue: 'Mushrooms have released their water in the first 10 minutes and then reabsorbed almost all of it — you can see the pan go from wet to nearly dry between the 10 and 20-minute checks; caps have gone deep mahogany-brown with slightly wrinkled skins; gill sides are dark brown and glossy from the released juices.',
    oilSprayRequired: false,
    proTip: 'Do not rinse mushrooms under running water — they are 90% water already, and a rinse loads them with even more that has to boil off before they can brown. A damp paper towel wipe is enough for grit. Roasting gill-side-up for the first 15 minutes lets the released moisture pool in the gill cup and evaporate off the top rather than steaming the cap side; the flip at 15 minutes puts the caps down for the final browning.',
    verificationBasis: 'USDA MyPlate does not set an internal-temperature target for roasted mushrooms — doneness is judged by the release-and-reabsorb liquid cycle plus deep-brown cap color, so the internal-temp field is omitted rather than guessed (HR-2). The 425°F / 20–25 minute window reflects standard high-heat roasting for whole 1.5–2 inch cremini on a rimmed half-sheet (Serious Eats "The Ultimate Roasted Mushrooms" and Cook\'s Illustrated "Roasted Mushrooms with Garlic and Thyme" both land in this window for the same cut and pan).',
    technicalDeepDive: 'Mushrooms are mostly water held inside a network of fungal cell walls, and that water has to leave before real browning can happen, since surface moisture absorbs incoming heat as latent energy instead of letting the surface climb into Maillard range. Roasting gill-side up for the first fifteen minutes gives the released liquid a cupped surface to pool and evaporate from, rather than letting it run down and steam the rounded cap the way it would if the caps sat down first. Once most of that water is gone, the sugars concentrated in the cell walls are finally free to brown, which is why the color shift happens late and fast rather than gradually, and why the flip is timed to follow the water loss rather than the clock.',
    keywords: ['how long to roast mushrooms', 'sheet pan cremini mushrooms', 'roasted baby bella temperature', 'whole mushrooms oven 425', 'roasted mushrooms release liquid'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to roast whole cremini mushrooms on a sheet pan: 425°F (218°C) for 20–25 minutes. Wipe, do not rinse; gill-side up, flip at 15.',
