    verificationBasis: 'Idaho Potato Commission "Baked Potato Doneness Guide" (205–210°F internal for full starch gelatinisation); tested in Ninja Foodi AF101 6-qt at 400°F for 12-oz russets. No poultry/ground-meat safe temp applies to potatoes, so no carryover exception needed.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A russet potato is a dense block of starch granules suspended in water, and full gelatinization — where those granules absorb water and swell into a fluffy structure — only completes once internal temperature holds in the low 200s, which is why the target sits at 205 to 210°F. Because the potato is a poor conductor with real mass, heat works inward layer by layer, so the center lags the surface for most of the 35 to 45 minutes even at a steady 400°F air temperature. The skin, with far less mass and constant exposure to moving air, dries out and crosses into Maillard browning long before the interior finishes.',
