    technicalDeepDive: 'Cauliflower\'s craggy, branching surface has far more nooks than a smooth vegetable, so the oil coating here isn\'t just flavor — it fills those gaps and gives the moving air better contact than the pockets of trapped humidity that would otherwise sit against bare floret skin. Pectin binds the fibrous stem more tightly than it does the branching curd, so the dense core needs a longer conductive path to soften than the outer flowerets need to brown, which is the real reason the midpoint shake matters: it swaps which pieces face the direct airflow so the slower-cooking interior of the batch gets equal exposure instead of staying pale.',
