    technicalDeepDive: 'A Brussels sprout is built like a miniature cabbage, with tightly wrapped leaf layers insulating a dense core, so the outer leaves — thin and directly exposed — lose moisture almost immediately and crisp into thin, brittle shards long before the packed interior softens. That core holds the sprout\'s sulfur-based glucosinolate compounds, which mellow out of their raw bitterness only once sustained heat has had time to break them down, so the 12 to 15 minute window is really calibrated to the center rather than the exterior, which finishes browning much sooner. The freshly cut face exposes sugars sealed under skin, and that surface caramelizes harder under direct airflow than anywhere skin remains intact.',
