    verificationBasis: 'Uno Pizzeria & Grill (original Chicago deep-dish recipe) published bake profile 475°F/25 min for 10-inch; Lodge Cast Iron "Deep Dish Pizza" recipe reference 475°F/22–28 min in 10-inch skillet; America\'s Test Kitchen "Chicago Deep-Dish Pizza" cookbook 500°F 20 min for equivalent volume — no probe target because pizza doneness is a crust colour + sauce reduction pair, not an internal-temp target, so internalTempTargetF is omitted per HR-2.',
    technicalDeepDive: 'A dough wall pressed an inch and a half up the sides of a greased skillet is effectively pan-frying along that whole vertical surface, not just baking, which gives the edge a fried character rather than a baked one. That much dough also takes time to heat through, and the bottom-rack position adds extra radiant energy from the oven floor, pushing heat into the base faster than surrounding air could manage for a load this thick. The crushed tomato on top is doing its own slow work meanwhile, losing water to the dry oven air and concentrating into a thick, reduced sauce rather than staying thin and watery.',
    relatedRecipeSlug: 'cast-iron-deep-dish-pizza',
    keywords: ['how long to bake deep dish pizza cast iron', 'chicago deep dish skillet time', 'cast iron pizza temperature', 'why is my deep dish soggy', 'deep dish crust golden'],
    metaDescription: 'How long to bake Chicago deep-dish pizza in a cast-iron skillet: 22–28 minutes at 475°F. Why the cheese layer goes under the sauce.',
