    technicalDeepDive: 'A strip loin has a tighter grain and less marbling than a ribeye, and because fat conducts heat more poorly than lean muscle, a leaner strip and a well-marbled ribeye of equal thickness don\'t heat through at quite the same rate. Basting the top face with hot butter in the closing minutes delivers heat through a liquid to the surface that never touches the skillet, acting like a gentle second sear from above. Because the steak is a full inch and a half thick, the crust holds enough heat on its own to keep conducting inward through the rest, which is why the center keeps rising after the pan work ends.',
