    technicalDeepDive: 'Broccoli\'s cell walls are thin and the floret structure is porous compared to a root vegetable or cut of meat, so steam heat conducts through it almost as fast as it reaches the surface, leaving very little gap between come-up-to-pressure and the vegetable actually being cooked. That speed is the entire risk: pectin in the cell walls starts breaking down as soon as the floret is enveloped in near-boiling steam, and with so little mass to slow that process, every extra second of exposure keeps softening cells that are already done. An immediate quick release is what arrests that softening right at the point a paring knife meets slight resistance instead of none.',
