    technicalDeepDive: 'Because these meatballs finished their real cooking before freezing, the physics here differs from a raw-meat cook: ice on each ball\'s surface must first absorb latent heat and revert to liquid, consuming energy without raising temperature, before any further warming can register at all. Submerging the frozen balls in marinara rather than steaming them on a trivet matters because liquid conducts and convects heat more efficiently around an irregular frozen surface than vapor does, part of why five minutes suffices versus the longer hold a frozen roast needs. The 160°F endpoint isn\'t pasteurizing raw meat the way it would fresh ground beef, it\'s confirming a previously cooked, frozen product has climbed back through the range where surface bacteria picked up in handling could otherwise multiply during storage.',
