    technicalDeepDive: 'Chicken breast is an extremely lean, fast-twitch muscle with almost no intramuscular fat to buffer moisture loss, so once its actin fibers pass their coagulation point they start squeezing out water, and every extra minute above that threshold costs more juiciness here than in a fattier cut. Baking hot and fast at 425°F shortens the total time the meat spends in that moisture-expelling zone compared with a slower bake, since the exterior reaches serving color while the interior is still climbing toward target. Pulling at 158°F relies on carryover: the breast is a solid, water-dense muscle mass, and heat already stored in its outer layers keeps conducting toward the center for several minutes, closing the gap to 165°F without added direct heat.',
