    technicalDeepDive: 'Convection ovens work by moving hot air across every exposed surface, but that only works if the air can actually reach the surface — crowd frozen fries onto one pan and the trapped air saturates with steam thrown off as the surface ice melts, so neighboring pieces braise each other instead of drying and browning. Splitting 2 pounds across two half-sheet pans keeps enough open space that moving air can sweep that steam away as fast as it forms, which is why the same 425°F setting that browns a spaced fry in this window leaves a crowded one pale even after the full 28 minutes. Once the surfaces run dry, radiant heat from above and convective heat from the moving air drive Maillard browning together, which is why color develops quickly once it finally starts.',
