    technicalDeepDive: 'Pulling the breast at 160°F and letting it coast to 165°F relies on carryover cooking: heat stored in the outer layers keeps conducting inward toward the cooler center for a few minutes after the pan, the same gradient-driven flow that cools any hot object toward its surroundings. Breast meat carries very little intramuscular fat or connective tissue compared with thigh, so once its myosin and actin have coagulated there is no buffer left protecting it from drying out, which is why timing the pull matters more here than in a fattier cut. Pounding the tapered raw breast to an even three-quarter inch matters for the same reason conduction always matters in a skillet: without it, heat reaches the thin end and finishes cooking well before the thick end catches up.',
