    technicalDeepDive: 'Carrots stay firm below roughly 183°F because that\'s near where the pectin gluing their cell walls together begins breaking down, so this braise needs sustained heat above that point for a real stretch of time rather than a quick blast, which is why 35 to 45 minutes under a lid at 375°F works better here than a fast, high-heat roast. Keeping the pot covered traps the butter, maple syrup, and stock as steam that condenses back onto the carrots, keeping the cut surfaces constantly wet with fat-rich liquid rather than drying out the way an open roasting pan would. Carrots\' orange color comes from beta-carotene, a compound that dissolves into fat rather than water, so cooking them directly in melted butter helps carry more of that color and flavor out of the cell walls than a dry roast would.',
