    technicalDeepDive: 'Grinding beef redistributes any surface bacteria throughout the meat, which is why ground beef carries a 160°F safe minimum instead of the 145°F allowed for a whole cut like a steak — there is no intact outer layer left to rely on for pathogen control. The 80/20 fat ratio matters mechanically too: at 425°F that fat begins liquefying before the lean portion\'s myoglobin finishes denaturing from red to brown, so rendered fat bastes the patty from within rather than the surface drying out first. At roughly a third of a pound, the patty is thin enough that conductive and convective heat from both faces can meet near the center within minutes, rather than requiring the longer indirect roast a thicker cut would need.',
