    technicalDeepDive: 'Skirt steak comes from the diaphragm and carries more intramuscular fat than flank, which helps it brown quickly and stay juicy under direct broiler heat, but it is also thinner, with a higher surface-area-to-volume ratio, so heat reaches the center almost as fast as it browns the crust. That is the physics behind the short six-to-eight-minute window at 500°F: there isn\'t enough mass in the steak to buffer a slow ramp, so the exterior must hit Maillard temperatures almost immediately or the whole piece overshoots 130°F before any real crust forms. Cutting the steak in half to fit the pan also helps, since a smaller, less crowded sheet heats up faster than one packed edge to edge.',
