    technicalDeepDive: 'Whole beets are dense, low-surface-area roots, so heat works inward almost entirely by conduction from the crock\'s hot wall and by the thin steam layer the quarter cup of water generates, which is why an unpeeled beet takes hours even on High to soften evenly to the center. Leaving the skin and an inch of stem attached does double duty: it keeps the vivid betalain pigments from leaching into the cooking liquid the way exposed flesh would, and it protects the thin cambium layer just beneath the surface that binds skin to flesh. Once that layer breaks down under sustained heat, the skin loosens enough to rub away with barely any pressure.',
