    technicalDeepDive: 'A bone-in chop cooks differently from a boneless one because bone conducts heat more slowly than surrounding muscle, so the center lags behind and is less likely to rocket past the 145°F point where pork\'s myosin and actin proteins set firm and start expelling moisture. Submerged in apple and onion juices rather than exposed to dry convection, the chop loses almost nothing to evaporation, so the lean loin muscle stays hydrated even as it crosses that coagulation threshold. The low, steady heat also gives the modest connective tissue running through the chop time to soften gradually instead of seizing, which is the usual failure mode when a lean cut like this is cooked fast and hot.',
