    technicalDeepDive: 'Curing brine already swelled the muscle fibers and boosted their water-holding capacity, so the smoker\'s real job is dissolving connective tissue rather than fighting moisture loss. A brisket flat is leaner and more sinewy than the point, laced with collagen that stays chewy until held well above its denaturation range long enough to hydrolyze into gelatin, which is why the target lands at 203°F instead of a medium finish. Hours of steady 250°F heat give that hydrolysis time to finish without flash-drying the exterior, since gradual heating lets moisture migrate outward near the rate it evaporates. Washing out curing salt beforehand lets the pepper and coriander crust dehydrate into bark instead of staying gummy.',
